  "title": "Tuvieron --- the Strong Stems, Made Plural",
  "drivablePrefix": 1,
  "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:8a59ec1855ed2387",
  "lessons": [
    {
      "id": "ES-C55-tuvieron",
      "sequence": 2245,
      "title": "tuvieron",
      "headword": "tuvieron",
      "romanization": "tuvieron",
      "gloss": "they had — the strong stems in the plural, where the stress finally moves back to the ending",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:8cc3fa4c8741033f",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Say the three strong pasts you know. (Tuve, hice, estuve.) Where does the stress fall? (On the stem — TU-ve, HI-ce.)"
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "That is what makes them strong. Now watch what the plural does to it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "grammar",
          "title": "Grammar Lens: the stem gives the stress back",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Strong stem + -ieron."
            },
            {
              "kind": "table",
              "headers": [
                "singular",
                "plural"
              ],
              "columns": 2,
              "rowCount": 3,
              "utterances": [
                "singular: tuve. plural: tuvieron.",
                "singular: hice. plural: hicieron.",
                "singular: estuve. plural: estuvieron."
              ]
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The ending is the same -ieron you learned for comer and vivir. The strong stem does not change — tuv-, hic-, estuv- — it simply takes the ordinary plural ending."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "And the stress moves: TU-ve, but tu-VIE-ron. The stem holds it in tuve and tuvo and hands it back here, because -ieron is two syllables and takes it."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Note that tuviste never had it — tu-VIS-te is stressed on the ending like any ordinary past. The stem only ever held the stress in two of the forms, which is exactly how much irregularity Spanish was willing to pay for."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "No written accent on any of them — they end in -n, so the default is already right, exactly as with hablaron."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
